--- learned
`filter` plus `includes` answers "which shows have this genre?". With `filter`, `map`, `find` and `includes` you can ask almost anything of a list. Tomorrow you'll build the clicks and typing yourself.
--- summary
`array.includes(value)` checks whether a list contains a value.
--- mistakes
- `show.genre` instead of `show.genres` (the key has an s).
- `show.genres === genre` (an array is never equal to one string).


=== step sort
title: Best first
minutes: 20
slot: js
bonus: yes
--- explain
`sort` puts a list in order. To sort by a number, give it a function that compares two items, `a` and `b`:

```js
const byPrice = [...snackList].sort((a, b) => a.price - b.price);
```

- If the answer is negative, `a` comes first; if it's positive, `b` comes first. `a.price - b.price` gives cheapest first; `b.price - a.price` gives the most expensive first.
- Careful: unlike `filter` and `map`, `sort` **changes the list you call it on**. `[...snackList]` makes a copy first, so the original stays as it is. The three dots are called **spread**: "take all the items and put them in a new array".
--- task
Write `sortByRating(list)` that returns a **copy** of the list, highest rating first. A new section, **Best first**, appears on the page.
--- hint
Copy with `[...list]`, then `.sort((a, b) => ...)`. For the highest first, subtract `a.rating` from `b.rating`.
--- hint
```js
function sortByRating(list) {
  return [...list].sort((a, b) => b.rating - a.rating);
}
```
--- solution-append
function sortByRating(list) {
  return [...list].sort((a, b) => b.rating - a.rating);
}
--- check
t.expect(!t.errors.length, `There's an error in the Console: "${t.errors[0]}". Read the help under it and fix that line.`);
const f = t.get("sortByRating");
t.expect(typeof f === "function", "I can't find a function called `sortByRating`. Start with `function sortByRating(list) {`.");
const input = [{ name: "A", rating: 7 }, { name: "B", rating: 9 }, { name: "C", rating: 8 }];
let r;
try { r = f(input); } catch (e) { t.fail("Your function crashed: " + e.message); }
t.expect(Array.isArray(r), "`sortByRating` should give back a list. Add `return`.");
const order = r.map((s) => s.name).join("");
t.expect(order !== "ACB", "That's lowest first. For the highest first, use `b.rating - a.rating`.");
t.expect(order === "BCA", `Sorting ratings 7, 9, 8 gave ${r.map((s) => s.rating).join(", ")}. It should give 9, 8, 7.`);
t.expect(input.map((s) => s.name).join("") === "ABC", "Your function changed the original list. Sort a copy: `[...list].sort(...)`.");
t.expect(t.get("shows")[0].name === "Friends", "The `shows` list itself got sorted. Sort a copy: `[...list].sort(...)`.");
t.expect(t.$$("#best-first li").length === 12, "The Best first section should list all 12 shows. Press **Run again** above the preview.");
--- learned
`sort` with a compare function orders a list by any number. Copying first with `[...list]` keeps the original safe.
--- summary
`[...list].sort((a, b) => b.x - a.x)` sorts a copy, biggest first.


=== step count
title: Count the genres
minutes: 20
slot: js
bonus: yes
--- explain
An object can also work as a set of counters. When the key is in a variable, use square brackets instead of a dot:

```js
const counts = {};
const word = "tea";
counts[word] = 1;           // same as counts.tea = 1
counts[word] = counts[word] + 1;
console.log(counts);        // {"tea":2}
```

A key that doesn't exist yet is `undefined`, and `undefined + 1` is `NaN` (*not a number*). So the first time you see a key, set it to 1; after that, add 1.

Each show has several genres, so you need a loop **inside** a loop: one through the shows, and one through each show's `genres`.
--- task
Write `countByGenre(list)` that returns an object like `{ Comedy: 4, Drama: 8, ... }`. Use loops and an object, not a new array method.

When it works, the genre buttons on the page show their counts.
--- hint
Start with `const counts = {};`. Loop through the shows; inside, loop through `show.genres`. For each genre: `if (counts[genre])` add 1, `else` set it to 1. Return `counts` after the loops.
--- hint
```js
function countByGenre(list) {
  const counts = {};
  for (const show of list) {
    for (const genre of show.genres) {
      if (counts[genre]) {
        counts[genre] = counts[genre] + 1;
      } else {
        counts[genre] = 1;
      }
    }
  }
  return counts;
}
```
--- solution-append
function countByGenre(list) {
  const counts = {};
  for (const show of list) {
    for (const genre of show.genres) {
      if (counts[genre]) {
        counts[genre] = counts[genre] + 1;
      } else {
        counts[genre] = 1;
      }
    }
  }
  return counts;
}
--- check
t.expect(!t.errors.length, `There's an error in the Console: "${t.errors[0]}". Check that every \`{\` has its \`}\`.`);
const f = t.get("countByGenre");
t.expect(typeof f === "function", "I can't find a function called `countByGenre`. Start with `function countByGenre(list) {`.");
let c;
try { c = f(t.get("shows")); } catch (e) { t.fail("Your function crashed: " + e.message); }
t.expect(c && typeof c === "object" && !Array.isArray(c), `\`countByGenre(shows)\` gives back ${JSON.stringify(c)}. Return the \`counts\` object after the loops.`);
t.expect(!Object.values(c).some((v) => Number.isNaN(v)), "Some counts are `NaN`. The first time you see a genre, set its count to 1 instead of adding 1 to `undefined`.");
t.expect(c.Comedy === 4 && c.Drama === 8, `\`countByGenre(shows)\` gives Comedy: ${c.Comedy}, Drama: ${c.Drama}. It should give Comedy: 4, Drama: 8. Loop through every genre of every show.`);
t.expect(c.Fantasy === 2 && Object.keys(c).length === 12, "Count every genre: there are 12 different ones in data.js.");
let e;
try { e = f([]); } catch (err) { t.fail("Your function crashed with an empty list: " + err.message); }
t.expect(e && Object.keys(e).length === 0, "`countByGenre([])` should give an empty object `{}`. Use the parameter `list`, not `shows`.");
t.expect(/Drama 8/.test(t.text("#genre-buttons")), "The genre buttons should show their counts, like Drama 8. Press **Run again** above the preview.");
--- learned
An object with keys you add as you go is a flexible counter. `counts[genre]` uses the value of `genre` as the key.
--- summary
`obj[key]` reads or sets a property whose name is in a variable.


=== step total
title: Watch everything
minutes: 15
slot: js
bonus: yes
--- explain
A loop can also add things up. Start a total at 0 with `let`, and add to it on every round:

```js
let total = 0;
for (const snack of snackList) {
  total = total + snack.price;
}
console.log(total);   // 7.5
```

It has to be `let`, because the total changes on every round. And it must be created **before** the loop: inside, it would start again at 0 each time.
--- task
Write `totalWatchTime(list)` that returns the total minutes needed to watch every episode of every show in the list (each show: episodes times runtime).

A new section, **Watch everything**, appears on the page.
--- hint
`let total = 0;`, then a loop that adds `show.episodes * show.runtime` to `total`. Return `total` after the loop.
--- hint
```js
function totalWatchTime(list) {
  let total = 0;
  for (const show of list) {
    total = total + show.episodes * show.runtime;
  }
  return total;
}
```
--- solution-append
function totalWatchTime(list) {
  let total = 0;
  for (const show of list) {
    total = total + show.episodes * show.runtime;
  }
  return total;
}
--- check
t.expect(!t.errors.length, `There's an error in the Console: "${t.errors[0]}". Read the help under it and fix that line.`);
const f = t.get("totalWatchTime");
t.expect(typeof f === "function", "I can't find a function called `totalWatchTime`. Start with `function totalWatchTime(list) {`.");
const shows = t.get("shows");
const want = shows.reduce((sum, s) => sum + s.episodes * s.runtime, 0);
let r;
try { r = f(shows); } catch (e) {
  t.expect(!/total is not defined/.test(e.message), "Your function crashed: `total` doesn't exist after the loop. Create it with `let total = 0;` before the loop, not inside it.");
  t.fail("Your function crashed: " + e.message);
}
t.expect(r !== undefined, "`totalWatchTime(shows)` gives back `undefined`. Return `total` after the loop.");
t.expect(r !== shows[shows.length - 1].episodes * shows[shows.length - 1].runtime, "You get only the last show's minutes. Create `total` with `let` before the loop, and add to it inside.");
t.expect(r === want, `\`totalWatchTime(shows)\` gives ${r}. It should give ${want} minutes: for each show, add episodes times runtime.`);
t.expect(f([]) === 0, "`totalWatchTime([])` should give 0. Start the total at 0.");
t.expect(f([{ episodes: 2, runtime: 30 }]) === 60, "Use the parameter `list` in your loop, not `shows`.");
t.expect(/655 hours/.test(t.text("#watch-all")), "The Watch everything section should appear. Press **Run again** above the preview.");
--- learned
A running total with `let` and a loop can add up anything. All twelve shows take 655 hours: more than 27 days without sleep.
--- summary
Add up a list: `let total = 0;` before the loop, `total = total + ...` inside.
